		<id>http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4473&amp;p=35126#p35126</id>
		<link href="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4473&amp;p=35126#p35126"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: Broken external speaker jack]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4473&amp;p=35126#p35126"><![CDATA[
I was able to fix it. For anyone else who might encounter the same issue, here's what I did.  I drilled a small vertical hole in the black plastic body of the jack, just beyond the point where the separator between the tip and ring would be when the plug is fully inserted. This exposed the tip contact in the jack. I probed around it with an unbent paper clip until I found a direction where the internal speaker came to life. It appeared that if I bent the contact a little towards the rear of the radio, it would again touch the contact that enables the internal speaker. A bit of pushing, and all was well again. So far, it's survived several insert/remove cycles. Much easier than removing the board and soldering in a replacement.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2447">KD7MW</a> — Mon Jan 13, 2025 4:29 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
